{
  "image_name": "c4e59ac0e78b17a2ef9daa8f6e0abb52.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes how semiconductor doping changes charge carriers and energy bands, and how these ideas apply to junctions and contacts. On the left, a silicon lattice is shown with substitutional dopants: phosphorus creates an n-doped material by contributing an extra electron, while boron creates a p-doped material by introducing a hole. The central band diagrams compare intrinsic, n-doped, and p-doped semiconductors. Doping adds donor or acceptor states near the band edges and shifts the Fermi level upward toward the conduction band for n-type material and downward toward the valence band for p-type material; a small occupancy plot at the side indicates the usual Fermi–Dirac filling around the Fermi level.\n\nThe lower-left panel shows a p–n junction at equilibrium. The conduction and valence bands bend across a depletion or space-charge region, producing a built-in potential that separates the n- and p-doped sides while the Fermi level remains flat.\n\nThe lower-right diagrams show metal–semiconductor contacts. A metal next to intrinsic semiconductor forms injection barriers for electrons and holes. With heavy n-doping, the barrier becomes narrow and the bands bend strongly, so carriers can tunnel through the interface, indicating an ohmic-like contact enabled by doping.",
